<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="serotonin and melatonin biosynthesis II" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Ubiquitous Plant Pathways (UPP) that was generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most land plants. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: N-ACETYL-5-METHOXY-TRYPTAMINE Melatonin| appears to be present in all organisms, including bacteria, animals, and plants, though its biological functions differ among species. The roles of melatonin in plants are mainly associated with its antioxidant activity |CITS: [22016420]|. |FRAME: SEROTONIN &quot;Serotonin&quot;| and |FRAME: N-ACETYL-5-METHOXY-TRYPTAMINE| are both derived from the amino acid |FRAME: TRP|. For the metazoan pathway see |FRAME: PWY-6030|. &lt;b&gt;About this Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The biosynthesis of serotonin and melatonin in plants and cyanobacteria differ from the pathway in animals in the two enzymes that produce |FRAME: SEROTONIN| from |FRAME: TRP|. While the metazoan pathway proceeds via |FRAME: 5-HYDROXY-TRYPTOPHAN|, the plant pathway forms |FRAME: TRYPTAMINE| as an intermediate |CITS: [21884550]|. Following the production of serotinin, the two pathways proceed similarly via |FRAME: N-ACETYL-SEROTONIN| to form |FRAME: N-ACETYL-5-METHOXY-TRYPTAMINE|. |FRAME: EC-2.3.1.87|, catalyzes the &lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;-acetylation of |FRAME: SEROTONIN| to |FRAME: N-ACETYL-SEROTONIN|, while |FRAME: EC-2.1.1.4| catalyzes the last step, which is also the limiting step in the pathway. Even though it has been documented that cyanobacteria produce melatonin, as of 2023 only one of the four enzymes (serotonin &lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;-acetyltransferase) has been identified.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="chawkins" />
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="caspi" />
  </Authors>
